  "worked_solution": "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $(x-6)(2x+3),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit. \n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $2x+3,$ and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $x-6:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y}{x-6}+\\frac{2y}{2x+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2x+3}{2x+3}\\cdot \\frac{4y}{x-6}+\\frac{x-6}{x-6}\\cdot \\frac{2y}{2x+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4y(2x+3)}{(x-6)(2x+3)}+\\frac{2y(x-6)}{(x-6)(2x+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4y(2x+3)+2y(x-6)}{(x-6)(2x+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{8xy+12y+2xy-12y}{(x-6)(2x+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{10xy}{(x-6)(2x+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
